<p>There they all sat, the whole senior class of Averagedale. As famous a person as Averagedale High could muster approached the podium. He cleared his throat and the mic squeaked with feedback.</p>

<p>“It’s the tradition,” Mr. Wannabe Famous began, “to deliver optimistic and inspiring graduation speeches. However, to be realistic, I have to say that these are dark times. Statistically speaking, two and half of you will find jobs and become financially independent. The rest?” He trailed off. “Stay with parents for life? Homeless? Yesterday tank drones evicted 10,000 homeless, right here in Averagedale. But maybe it doesn’t matter who finds work and who doesn’t. Two days ago Long Island was swamped by an out of season hurricane. Which was probably caused by climate change, unless you don’t believe in climate change. But maybe it doesn’t matter what happens with the weather. We all know the nuclear holocaust is only a few years away.</p>

<p>What I can say is, even though things may look grim, you will find your way. In my experience, there is always a way. When I was your age…” Ravenna zoned out, staring at the ass her history teacher. She flipped perspectives, and saw through the eyes of her favorite bird: a three-year old raven, Squeakie. She was sitting on the roof the school auditorium. All her birds were there, except two, the eagle and the sparrow, who must be out foraging. She launched herself from the edge of the roof, coasted for a bit, flapped to gain altitude. Her immense, strong wings rejoiced in the exercise and the sun. This morning the air was soft, but also a bit wavy. This was almost her favorite kind of air. It tickled her feathers. A message from the daemon she’d left operating her human body: lots of noise and movement. She flipped back. Her human body was sitting impossibly still with perfect posture. The others were standing up. Chairs scraped. She stood up too.</p>

<p>“Aahmed Ahammed,” called the principal. Ravenna did loopty-loops in Squeakie’s body while she waited for her name.</p>

