<h2 id="2-lost-in-cyberspace">2: Lost in Cyberspace</h2>

<p>Ravenna shuffled aimlessly past refrigerators, canned soup and surfboards. Keys jangled in her purse. Charlie’s credit card was silent, but she stood 2 inches taller because of it. She entered the software section. Rubber handles sat on the shelves. Shoppers picked up different handles, turned them over, put them down again. With augmented reality disabled, the whole scene looked ridiculous.</p>

<p>Ravenna enabled MegaMart’s augmented reality layer. 10 meter tall flashing coupons blinded her. She activated some ad filters. Now aisle labels seemed to float in the air, superimposed on her normal vision. On her left were aisles of regulator programs. There was an aisle of romantic programs, similar to what she’d improvised in Charlie’s regulator that first morning in his place. She could get a program for her regulator to make her interested in having three children. There were programs to make her a faithful long-term partner, programs to make her like her partner’s snoring, programs to make her like Tantric sex. The family aisle was next. Name any relation, there was a program for loving that person more. There was an aisle of regulator programs for companies to give their employees, to make them loyal or hardworking or creative. In the educational aisle were programs for motivation in sports, music and academics. In the health isle, she could lose weight, always go to the gym, and adhere perfectly to a variety of diets. She could become devoted to any major world religion, and quite a few she’d never heard of. She could count her blessings, pray or meditate for as many minutes as she wanted every day without fail. In the political section, she had the choice of becoming a patriot of 100 different nations.</p>

<p>Ravenna went back to the health isle, picked up a yoga regulator program. In her vision the rubber handle was covered by a swarm of images. A gorgeous blond in revealing elastic stood on one hand next to a cliff in front of a sunset. A very small window opened onto 33 pages of legal information. Reviews, testimonials, measurements and certifications. A huge INSTALL NOW button. The thing was free! Her stomach flipped over. How did they make money? Product placement? She slammed the handle back on the shelf as if she could be contaminated just by looking at it. What would they make her buy if she installed it? Or did the authors have some even subtler scheme in mind?</p>

