<p>Ravenna didn’t hear him. Plans blossomed in her brain.</p>

<hr />

<p>Ravenna sent her hawk to find a mouse. The other birds fanned out over the sprawl of low-rise apartments and duplexes, trying the ten most common passwords on every home wifi they encountered. In no time at all, she had a live mouse and access to two home networks. She ordered two simple computers with same-day delivery, each the size of a deck of cards. With the birds it would be no problem to change the batteries inconspicuously. The birds dropped one in the bushes at each house, and she set up a proxy scheme: her phone connected to 214 5th St, the computer there routed to a legally ambiguous satellite that was outside all jurisdictions, the satellite routed to 11 Ashen Ave, and from there to the wider world. Anyone monitoring the communications of Ravenna, or of robots controlled from Ashen, would find nothing suspicious to investigate. And if they did investigate, the authorities could find no legal entity to subpoena.</p>

<p>Charlie had a stash of SMIs, the implant that was in her, Hilo, and the birds. They were in sterile packages in the hallway closet. He installed an SMI and a small light in the mouse, and at night the hawk dropped the mouse at 11 Ashen Ave. Ravenna switched to the body of the mouse, left her human body playing a recorded exercise routine.</p>

<p>Air tickled the mouse’s fur as the hawk took off. The smell of wet earth, of dog. The sound of a television, across the street. She moved a paw forward experimentally. She heard the sound of her tiny claws on a slate roof. She trotted down to the edge of the roof, and climbed upside down under the eaves. She walked along the bottom of the eaves, seeing nothing. For a moment, her claw sounds stopped to echo from the wall. A hole. She crawled inside, chewed effortlessly through the insulation, followed some empty space around a heating duct. She chewed through the ticky tack walls, emerged at floor level in the bedroom. Perfect. Two sounds of breathing. She found a bedside table, climbed on top. The breathing sounds had not changed pace. Wallet on the bedside table. Even more perfect. She pawed out each card, and turned on the light just for a moment, to see the card with the mouse’s eyes. In all, she photographed a driver’s license, two signed credit cards and a gym membership, all in the name of Susannah Smith. Fuck yeah. She backed out the mouse, and the hawk picked it up.</p>

<p>Ms. Smith, or at least her legal and financial part, ordered a tele-operated surgeon, and a humanoid bipedal robot typically used as a go-for by the shy, handicapped, or morbidly obese. The next day, the bipedal delivered itself to 11 Ashen Ave. A small computer in the bushes connected to the bipedal, and recited Ms. Smith’s birthday and mother’s maiden name. The bipedal recognized the small computer in the bushes as its owner, and a moment later Ravenna occupied its body.</p>

<p>The bipedal was horrible. Usually, Ravenna’s brain sent signals towards her spinal cord, which the SMI piped off instead to the spinal cord of a bird or a mouse or whatever. And the sensory signals from whatever body she was occupying were pasted in place of the sensory signals from her human body. Easy peasy lemony squeezy. But Ravenna did not see through the bipedal’s cameras: she watched a video of what it saw. And she did not walk with the legs of the bipedal, but twitched a joystick to guide the poor lost thing around the world. Despite the awkwardness, she picked up the package with the surgeon, and went to wait in a drone parking lot and charging station.</p>

<p>After all the preparation, Ravenna waited two weeks for Hilo to go hiking.</p>

<hr />

<p>The birds tracked the progress of Charlie towards the summit of Blint’s Bluff trail. There was a twenty something eating lunch, looking out over the clearcut stumps. The young woman left the cliffside, went into the bushes for a piss. The hawk circled above her, carrying in its talons the tele-operated surgeon. She pulled down her pants and squatted. The hawk dove from behind her dropped the surgeon onto her neck. She yelped in surprise, then was tranquilized.</p>

<p>Charlie sat at his desk in his apartment. Four side-by-side monitors showed the camera feed from the surgeon. Charlie’s hands danced over joysticks. Ravenna left the hawk for a moment, came to stand behind Charlie with her hands on his shoulders. He incised just below the hairline, where the sutures would be almost hidden by the girl’s curly golden locks. He worked quickly, separating the brain stem from the top the spinal cord. He inserted the SMI before her breathing had a chance to stop. “Go go go,” said Charlie. Ravenna jumped into the body of the girl.</p>

<p>The delay was noticeable, as the signals were routed through the home wifi and outer space. She stood up, as thoughtlessly as standing up in her own body, but she didn’t feel herself stand up, or see her point of view change, until a moment later.</p>

<p>Hilo and Ravenna, when they were younger and still on good terms, had spent days playing with their SMIs. They switched bodies and didn’t tell anyone, swapping lives for days on end. Other times they floated together in a senseless world, blind, deaf and unfeeling, but hearing the other’s voice as soon as the other made the intention to speak. They sculpted virtual sandcastles with imaginary hands, made whole universes from cloud and diamond and wishful thinking. They wrote crude programs to control two bodies with one mind.</p>

<p>Hilo and Ravenna were habituated from birth to their SMIs. They changed bodies as thoughtlessly as most people move their limbs. They scanned the local network as naturally as hearing. They had wreaked havoc in the school’s computers, watching with mice as the principal typed his password. Then, while their bodies sat in math class, they gave themselves straight A’s and randomly shuffled the grades of the other students. They replaced the online textbooks with <em>101 Stupid Jokes</em>, and deleted all the homework, without moving a muscle.</p>

<p>Aside from the delay, there was something clumsy about the hiker girl’s body. The hiker could keep the implant for a million years and never become as natural as Hilo.</p>

<p>The hawk, left to its instincts, took to the sky. Ravenna walked back to the plantless, rocky area near the bluff, and sat down, pretending to enjoy the view. Hilo huffed up the steep final climb. His phone claimed to be a cell phone tower, and the hiker girl’s phone routed its traffic through him. Surely his daemons were watching for suspicious traffic. Animals with implants, traffic sent through legally ambiguous satellites, things of that nature. But turnabout was fair play. The hiker girl’s SMI was claiming to be a regulator, receiving its orders from 11 Ashen Ave.</p>

<p>Hilo sat down a few yards away. He was 16, with messy brown hair, and athletic clothing with huge logos stamped all over the place. “Heya,” he said, pulling a water bottle from his bag.</p>

<p>“‘Sup,” she said. “Hey, you look familiar. Just got this itchy feeling I’ve seen you somewhere before.”</p>

<p>“Yeah,” he said. “I was in the news a lot, couple years ago. Implant slavery story.”</p>

<p>“Oh! I’m so sorry. That must have been horrible. I didn’t mean-“</p>

<p>“No, it’s ok,” he said. “It’s old news now.”</p>

<p>“Does it still bother you?” she asked.</p>

<p>“The thing that still gets me, is they paroled the fucking psychopath who did it. I mean, we both have autistic range empathy scores, which is why they killed the research program. But she was a real psychopathic psychopath. Teleop bird drops out of the sky, slices you open, fucks you up at the hardware level. Like a horror movie or something. But they could only prove that she did it with me. They couldn’t nail her for any of the others. So she’s still out there. If you ever meet a bird an implant, cover your neck and run.”</p>

<p>“Holy shit. I mean, wow. Once she got your implant, what did she do with you?”</p>

<p>“She just wanted to talk, mostly. I was scared and pissed, though. Not super talkative.”</p>

<p>“She just wanted to talk. Autistic range empathy scores. She must have been so lonely.”</p>

<p>“Nothing can justify what she did.”</p>

<p>“Of course not! I hope you don’t mind all these questions, I’m just so curious. I’m writing a thesis about the sociological implications of regulators, and obviously North Korea and the ever looming slavery threat is a big part. Have you ever spoken to her since?”</p>

<p>“No.” Hilo looked at his feet, pushed dirt around with his shoes. “We still went to high school together, after the parole. I managed to completely avoid her. Like most people avoided her. She graduated a month ago. I hope she’s dead in a ditch.”</p>

<p>“It’s a real shame, to waste your energy hating someone like that. You could enjoy the view, or the sunshine, but instead your thoughts go again and again to your fear and anger. A real shutting down.”</p>

<p>“Yeah, thanks wise lady. But what do I do?”</p>

<p>“Maybe just drop her a line. She what’s up to, now. You say you don’t have much empathy, but in case it reaches you, I bet she would really like that. And just reaching out will help you open up, be a bit less stuck inside.”</p>

<p>Hilo scuffed the dirt with his shoe some more.</p>

<p>“Just my advice, you know,” said Ravenna. “Take it or leave it, as you please. Lovely view isn’t it? They say that up here the air is a bit less polluted.”</p>

<p>“Yeah, it’s a great view,” said Hilo without looking. He turned, and started back down the mountain, seeming preoccupied.</p>

<p>Ravenna waited for Hilo to be far enough away. Her favorite part was coming. The police interrogators had asked her what she did with her other slaves. She had lied of course, said there were no other slaves. A bird confirmed that Hilo would see and hear nothing. Ravenna walked to edge of the cliff, hit a wall of vertigo. Instinctual fear hammered at her reptile brain, which could not understand that she was safe in Charlie’s apartment. She walked through the fear, took a step off the cliff. The ground rushed towards her. The breeze really cut down on the humid feeling in the air. Her legs and hips shattered. She filtered out the pain with a quick command. It was quite an interesting sensation, dying. She’d never felt her internal organs before, but now she could feel them touching the ground. Warm blood gushed everywhere. An eerie stillness: the absence of her breath, the absence of her heartbeat. The hiker’s nerve endings continued signaling, minutes after brain death.</p>

<p>Ravenna jumped out out of the experience, into the hawk. She brought the surgeon, who removed the incriminating SMI from the hiker’s body. The hawk clawed up the incision, rendering it quite unsurgical-looking. Ravenna flipped back to her normal body. She was a bit hungry.</p>

<h2 id="special-delivery">Special Delivery</h2>

<p>A week later, Ravenna was mindlessly sculpting a paperclip and zoning out. A daemon that identified relevant messages blipped at her. Ravenna looked at the notice. It was from a high-end search engine, the kind used by private investigators and online research agencies. One of her aliases had been mentioned in a file dumped to a cheap, public storage service. Her heart skipped a beat. She’d last used that alias two years ago, with Hilo. She paid off the search engine, got the location of the file. It was big. She downloaded it. It was encrypted. One of her private keys decrypted it. She had a daemon try opening it with different programs. It was an SMI recording. She jumped in, re-experiencing whatever had been recorded.</p>

<p>She was huge, and moving very fast over the ground. Air streamed through her. What the fuck? The network was crowded and confused. Parts of the network were inside her but not part of her. She heard pings coming from around and inside her. Like when she’d been a bat. Echolocation? No, radar. Radar represented as sound. She wasn’t a human or a bird. She was an airplane. No. A fleet of planes. She wasn’t huge, she was spread out. The planes were strange; their wings were swept forward instead of back. They turned on a dime, the same way that fruit flies change direction suddenly, but supersonic. Those were the planes in front. The planes in back were bigger, slower more conventional, full of network activity. The visuals gave Ravenna a headache, because there was no perspective and every depth was in focus. It was a sort of overview, that crazily zoomed in and out in response to the recorded eye movements. The landscape was sandy and shrubby, rolling hills. She was approaching a city. Seemingly at random, the earth would fountain up, cascading off something underground. Rockets streamed from the earth fountains. Some exploded into clouds of shrapnel; others chased her various planes, who darted and dodged and sometimes went down in fireballs. When she lost a plane, she didn’t feel pain, but her sense of body became smaller. Her recorded movements ebbed and flowed in the fleet of planes, scattering them from around a missile, congregating them around a target. Her body had complex reflexes, dodging and attacking on its own, which just added to Ravenna’s disorientation. Missiles and bullets dripped from her like sweat, stopping the flow of fire from the earth fountains. Long text messages with many acronyms were pasted on top of her vision. She constantly vocalized replies. Ravenna strained to decipher the messages. General Adams wanted her to destroy the radar installation on the next hill. The CIA said that there was a blogger in a small house on the next hill, who was heavily influencing elections in Albania. The NSA said that they had hacked into the city’s electrical grid and shut off all non-emergency power. The NSA also said that the CIA might have been hacked and might be sending forged messages. General Clover said that human rights NGOs were monitoring by satellite for civilian casualties, and certain congressmen must be reelected under all circumstances and could not take any negative press. General Adams wanted to start a voice call.</p>

<p>Ravenna lost most of her body, shrinking down to a just a kernel of larger planes in the back. She was divided in two by a wall of static and jamming. A line of earth fountains had not shot missiles, but poked antennas above ground. She watched the darting, weaving planes in the front start to divide, a part firing rockets at the antennas, another part continuing towards the city. The slower planes drove forward through the wall of static, into direct sunlight. Her head was burning, and the slower planes one by one caught fire. She was through the wall of static, and momentarily reunited with all her disperse parts. The strange sensorium fell away. Now she was a human. A human she would recognize anywhere. Hilo. A plane spiraled  away from her, caught fire. The earth turned around and around her, coming closer. The recording stopped.</p>

<p>Charlie was standing over her, looking concerned. “You’re shaking! Are you ok? You’re all white. What are you watching?”</p>

<p>The fear, nausea and adrenaline the recording evoked faded away. Ravenna bubbled with excitement. “This is amazing! Look, look…”  Charlie could not experience the recorded sensorium, but Ravenna made a conventional digital video from the audio and visual channels. The whole thing was only a minute and a half long.</p>

<p>Charlie didn’t get it at all. “Is this real?” he asked.</p>

<p>“No. The messages from the generals talk about international press coverage for this battle. Not a covert operation. And there’s been nothing like this in the news. So it’s a movie.”</p>

<p>“So Hilo is making a war movie in his spare time, and he shares it with you, because the hiking sweetheart whispered sweet nothings in his ear.”</p>

<p>“You’re not even close, Watson. Use that genius head of yours. When a movie company makes a war movie with lots of special effects, they burn millions of dollars and hire whole teams and it takes years. That’s just audio and visual. To get a whole sensorium experience going, even for a minute and a half, is a huge project. And you neglected to give us decent movie-editing software for our SMIs, so Hilo would have to start by making all his own editing programs. Which would take another fistful of million dollar bills. No, Hilo is certainly not behind this, although he did quite possibly send it to me.”</p>

<p>“Nice try, Ravenna, but no one is going to spend millions and hire whole teams to make a movie that only you and Hilo can watch.”</p>

<p>“Well, let’s see, which parts of the government could possibly be interested in an implant that makes you great at controlling flying things, and turns one out of two experimental subjects into trigger-happy psychopaths? The whole military and intelligence apparatus has probably been drooling over Hilo and I since we were born. Who paid for us, by the way?”</p>

<p>Charlie looked uncomfortable.</p>

<p>“Right,” continued Ravenna. “And we even come with regulators pre-installed. Why am I not already in love with my country? Does that subprogram auto-activate at a certain age? Do I really have the root password for my regulator? Maybe some underpaid clerk in the Pentagon can push a button and make me start singing “Star Spangled Banner”.”</p>

<p>“Ouch. We gave you guys a fair deal, as best we could. We did get backing from DARPA, sure, but it was basically a university research program. Both your implants gave you a genuine root password when you turned 18. Your parents were trying to help you, thought you could for sure find work if you were one of a handful adapted to the SMI from birth. The empathy thing was really an accident, not sponsored in any way. Honestly, I wanted to keep going anyway, but the ethics committee wouldn’t let us. I thought maybe DARPA would keep going, more discretely. If they did, I wasn’t in on the deal.”</p>

<p>“Wait, wait, this is starting to come together… So DARPA keeps going secretly. They make fancy planes that fly in flocks controlled by kids with SMIs. But, the hard fork in OpenDefense happens sooner. Before DARPA’s hatchlings are grown up enough. So they become interested in Hilo and me. Either they’re interested in me, and the movie is some sort of weird recruiting attempt, or they’re interested in Hilo, and he sends the movie to me for reasons I haven’t sorted out yet. Or maybe a sick combination of the two.”</p>

<p>“Yeah! And, wait, it’s not a movie. It’s a video game. Look, it keeps increasing in difficulty. At first Hilo is cruising towards the city, handling the resistance. Then the chatter goes nuts, and he has to fight and hold multiple correspondences, and sort out who to trust. Then there’s the wall of jamming antennas. Then the solar concentrator satellites burning planes. Hilo does the heroic thing, not the smart thing: he flies forward, so the small dodgy planes in front don’t waste their effort on the antenna wall. He comes out from beneath cloud cover in a slow plane, gets roasted, ejects into the heart of enemy territory. Heroism is easy in simulation. A way to measure skill, and see how people respond in sticky situations.”</p>

<p>“And at the end, the human body feels exactly like Hilo’s. So if it is a movie, then the makers used a recording specifically of Hilo’s body. And if it’s a game, then Hilo made a recording of himself playing, squirrels it away in a secret place, posts it with my alias when the NSA isn’t looking…”</p>

<p>“If Hilo really did post it without being coerced, what is he trying to say? Why couldn’t he just write you a nice letter the conventional way?”</p>

<p>“Because in words you can lie. The movie cannot be forged without a huge budget. Beyond a doubt, somebody has kindly taken the trouble to inform me that somebody is still pouring money into SMIs. Mr. Sender is not revealing this invaluable state secret for free. Mr. Sender expects that this movie will motivate me to do something, and whatever they think I’ll do I can figure out just from the movie. Mr. Sender knows about secret research programs.”</p>

<p>“Mr. Sender could be… Pentagon? Other OECD nation? Russia? China? A DAO state?”</p>

<p>“Could be. Maybe they want me to fly planes. But then they just spirit me away with a black bag over my head, switch out my regulator for a state-controlled model, then I do whatever they want and do it gladly. Weird movie not necessary.”</p>

<p>“Mr. Sender could be Hilo…”</p>

<p>“Suits with black sunglasses and earpieces show up at Hilo’s place, propose him to play a video game for a couple minutes. Suits go away, leaving Hilo’s regulator intact for unclear reasons. Maybe he didn’t perform well enough. Hilo figures out what’s up from the nature of the game. Hilo does not want to be brainwashed into a patriotic robot. Now he is so scared of the suits, that he even reaches out to me.”</p>

<p>“And why do you do something?”</p>

<p>“I never could leave Hilo well enough alone.”</p>

<p>“Yeah, but why?”</p>

<p>“We’ve been here before, remember? My lonely, you all empathetic and listeny.”</p>

<p>“Yeah, but why Hilo specifically?”</p>

<p>“He’s the only other one like me. My one true brother.”</p>

<p>“Maybe Hilo has been one up on you this whole time. Maybe Hilo hacked your regulator, years ago, until you became so obsessed that, ironically, you tried to slave him. Then he let you go. But you’re still obsessed. Old habits die hard. Or maybe he hasn’t let you go.”</p>

<p>Ravenna couldn’t think of a single word to say.</p>
